## Continuity summary — chat `69fd0f07-8a28-8395-8f3d-cb6d758965d7` Source reviewed: uploaded `69fd0f07-8a28-8395-8f3d-cb6d758965d7.2.task.show`. fileciteturn0file0 ## Current state The latest **built candidate** is: ```text chatgpt_claudecode_workflow_v0.0.215.zip ``` But the last protocol smoke showed the request still used: ```text current_baseline: chatgpt_claudecode_workflow_v0.0.214.zip current_version: v0.0.214 target_version: v0.0.216 ``` So `v0.0.215` appears to be installed/running, but **not yet adopted as the current artifact/source baseline** before the smoke. The next action is therefore **not** to design `v0.0.216` immediately. First adopt/verify `v0.0.215`, then rerun the protocol smoke. ## Architecture direction Promptbranch is being developed as a constrained Claude-Code-like workflow shell: ```text Promptbranch CLI / local host = control plane ChatGPT Project = workspace ChatGPT chat = task/session Project Sources = repo snapshots/specs/logs/release ZIPs MCP = deterministic local tools Ollama = optional proposal/summarization only Skills = reusable operating procedures ZIP artifacts = release outputs ``` The core rules remain: ```text - Backend-first reads. - Transactional writes. - State updates only after verified persistence. - ZIP root must contain repo contents directly. - No wrapper folder. - No cache/temp/log/generated files in ZIPs. - No patch artifacts. - Ollama cannot execute tools directly. - Skills cannot bypass policy gates. ``` The Ask/Reply Protocol work was added because Promptbranch still had a manual gap: ChatGPT generated a ZIP link, then the operator manually downloaded, verified, migrated, tested, adopted, and continued. The intended flow is now structured ask → structured reply envelope → artifact candidate → explicit download/verify/migrate → guarded test/adopt → next ask from adopted baseline. fileciteturn4file1 ## Key release timeline ### v0.0.189 Accepted after upload verification and full validation. It passed live upload, browser suite, agent suite, package hygiene, version consistency, and clean repo-state validation. fileciteturn4file0 ### v0.0.190 Built to fix artifact hygiene after v0.0.189 had included an accidental generated task transcript. Full test/report passed, but state still pointed to v0.0.189 until the later adoption/reconciliation work. ### v0.0.191 Added safe artifact adoption/reconciliation: ```bash pb artifact adopt <zip> --from-project-source --json ``` This allowed local Promptbranch state and artifact registry to be updated to an already-present Project Source without mutating Project Sources again. After this, the important distinction was: ```text v0.0.190 = adopted Project Source/artifact baseline v0.0.191 = latest accepted code/tooling release ``` ### v0.0.192–v0.0.200 Focused mostly on release-control hardening, baseline/current-state clarity, candidate/adoption mechanics, package hygiene, release-log handling, repair release handling, and safer release-control behavior. Important repair point: ```text v0.0.198 had a release-control shell/Python parsing defect. v0.0.198.1 / v0.0.198.2 were repair releases. v0.0.199 continued from v0.0.198.2. ``` ### v0.0.201–v0.0.207 Implemented the Ask/Reply Protocol and Artifact Intake MVP slices: ```text v0.0.201 = MVP-F0 ask/reply protocol foundation v0.0.202 = MVP-F1 artifact candidate extraction v0.0.203 = MVP-F2 explicit artifact download v0.0.203.1 = repair for file source overwrite detection v0.0.204 = MVP-F3 artifact inbox ZIP verification v0.0.205 = MVP-F4 candidate migration v0.0.206 = MVP-F5 guarded candidate test/adopt v0.0.207 = MVP-F6 ask from adopted baseline ``` These implemented the structured protocol pipeline but kept adoption guarded and explicit. The design explicitly says downloaded artifacts are never immediately accepted; they move through candidate → downloaded → verified → migrated → tested → adopted. fileciteturn4file1 ### v0.0.208–v0.0.210 Focused on controlled `pb ask --protocol` behavior: ```text v0.0.208 = protocol/plan flow cleanup v0.0.209 = controlled ask execution v0.0.210 = protocol timeout handling ``` ### v0.0.211 Added stale-answer detection. This stopped the system from parsing an old assistant answer as if it belonged to the latest `pb ask`. ### v0.0.212 Added fresh-turn verification, forced post-submit refresh/polling, and better failure JSON. The issue persisted: after submit, no fresh user turn became visible. ### v0.0.213 Added browser-level ask submit evidence, but the smoke failed earlier as a service/client read timeout, so submit evidence was not returned. ### v0.0.214 Fixed timeout-layer handling and partial submit-evidence return. The failure improved to: ```text status: ask_submission_not_visible submit_evidence.status: ... ``` So `v0.0.214` proved the service could return useful submit evidence instead of timing out. ### v0.0.215 Built from `v0.0.214` to harden submit visibility and transcript-refresh diagnostics only. Changes included: ```text - broader browser DOM user-turn selectors - generic conversation-turn probes - before/after generic turn counts - request_id / prompt-prefix detection across generic snippets - no-cache backend conversation detail fetches - clearer classifications: - ask_clicked_composer_cleared_dom_selector_missed - ask_clicked_composer_cleared_backend_stale - ask_clicked_but_no_turn_materialized ``` Validation from extracted ZIP passed: ```text 254 focused tests passed py_compile OK ZIP CRC/testzip OK artifact verify OK VERSION = v0.0.215 entry_count = 201 hygiene_violation_count = 0 wrapper_folder = false/null ``` But full live validation/adoption was not yet confirmed in the uploaded chat. ## Current failure pattern The latest smoke command was: ```bash pb ask "Protocol smoke only. Return a valid promptbranch.ask.reply envelope with status no_artifact. Do not create a ZIP." \ --protocol \ --from-current-baseline \ --target-version v0.0.216 \ --parse-reply \ --protocol-timeout-seconds 120 \ --protocol-fresh-turn-timeout-seconds 60 \ --protocol-fresh-turn-poll-seconds 2 \ --json \ | tee .pb_profile/release_logs/v0.0.215/pb_ask_protocol_smoke.v0.0.215.json ``` Observed diagnosis: ```text status: ask_clicked_composer_cleared_backend_stale clicked: true button_visible: true button_enabled: true composer_cleared: true after_composer.text_length: 0 before_user_turns.count: 0 generic_turns.count: 0 request_id_found: false message_count_delta: 0 ``` Interpretation: ```text Browser submit appears successful. Composer clears. But DOM/backend transcript still does not expose a fresh user turn. ``` So the problem is now likely **backend transcript visibility / conversation refresh / wrong transcript source**, not the send-button click itself. ## Immediate next step Run adoption/validation for `v0.0.215` first: ```bash ./chatgpt_claudecode_workflow_release_control.sh \ --version v0.0.215 \ --tests-only \ --adopt-if-green \ --skip-docker-logs \ --prune-release-logs \ --release-log-keep 12 ``` Then verify: ```bash pb artifact current --json | python3 -m json.tool ``` Expected: ```text artifact_ref: chatgpt_claudecode_workflow_v0.0.215.zip source_ref: chatgpt_claudecode_workflow_v0.0.215.zip code_version_matches_state_source: true ``` Then rerun the protocol smoke targeting `v0.0.216`. ## If it still fails after proper adoption Then build `v0.0.216` narrowly around: ```text backend transcript refresh / latest-message source hardening ``` Scope should be limited to: ```text - do not trust stale ask_response.answer - after composer clears, force backend transcript refresh through the same source used by pb task messages list - compare backend message IDs before/after, not just count - include latest backend message IDs and timestamps in JSON - distinguish: - submit_visible_but_backend_snapshot_stale - submit_visible_but_conversation_not_updated - task_reader_using_wrong_conversation - no artifact download - no migration - no adoption - no Project Source mutation ``` ## Verdict ✅ Strengths — release flow, artifact hygiene, candidate/adoption pipeline, and protocol diagnostics have become much stronger. ⚠️ Weaknesses — `pb ask --protocol --parse-reply` is still not green in live use; current failure is transcript freshness/visibility after apparent submit. 🔍 Unknowns — whether `v0.0.215` passes the same smoke after it is actually adopted as current baseline. 🧩 Next step — adopt/verify `v0.0.215`, rerun the protocol smoke, and only then decide whether `v0.0.216` is needed.

