Implemented `v0.0.217` from: ```text chatgpt_claudecode_workflow_v0.0.216.zip ``` ## Critical assessment The uploaded `session_20260515_113544.log` confirmed the key issue: ```text status: submit_visible_but_wrong_conversation expected_conversation_id: 69fd0f07-8a28-8395-8f3d-cb6d758965d7 actual_conversation_id: 6a062d16-35f4-8387-9217-1d2bd9dde63f ``` `v0.0.216` correctly detected the wrong conversation, but still included the wrong conversation’s assistant answer in `ask_response`. That is unsafe because a stale valid-looking answer could accidentally be parsed. ## What changed in `v0.0.217` - Added a strict protocol conversation lock. - `pb ask --protocol --parse-reply` now refuses to parse assistant text if the service/browser response points to a different conversation than the request target. - Added explicit failure status: ```text submit_clicked_target_conversation_lost ``` - Added error type: ```text conversation_lock_mismatch ``` - Fresh-turn evidence now only counts as fresh if the transcript source matches the expected conversation id. - No changes to: - artifact download - artifact migration - adoption - Project Source mutation - MCP/write capability ## Validation Passed from extracted ZIP: ```text VERSION = v0.0.217 py_compile OK 63 focused tests passed ZIP CRC/testzip OK entry_count = 201 hygiene_count = 0 wrapper_folder = false ``` One broader `pytest tests` attempt was not usable in this sandbox because `tests/test_api.py` tried to reach a service hostname and failed with name resolution. The focused protocol/version/container/MCP tests passed. ## Release artifact [Download `chatgpt_claudecode_workflow_v0.0.217.zip`](sandbox:/mnt/data/chatgpt_claudecode_workflow_v0.0.217.zip) ## Next step Install/test `v0.0.217`, then rerun the protocol smoke targeting `v0.0.218`.

