[110] assistant
Yes — your instinct is correct. I would **not call it merely cosmetic anymore**. It is **not a protocol-validation failure**, but it is serious enough that I would fix it **before accepting `v0.0.217` as the baseline**. ## Critical assessment The good part: ```text status: reply_validated reply_status: no_artifact reply_validation_ok: true artifact_candidate_count: 0 ``` So the validated protocol path succeeded. The risky part: ```text ask_response.answer: "Thinking Streaming interrupted. Waiting for the complete message..." ``` The uploaded smoke JSON shows the DOM/browser stream saw the request turn and then a short “Streaming interrupted” assistant surface, while the validated protocol reply exists elsewhere in the final parsed output. fileciteturn11file1 That creates a bad invariant: ```text Top-level protocol result says: validated reply ask_response.answer says: incomplete stream placeholder ``` That is dangerous because future tooling or operators may read `ask_response.answer` and make the wrong conclusion. ## Why this matters The Ask/Reply design says automation should use the validated JSON envelope, not human-readable or incidental answer text. It also says invalid, ambiguous, or inconsistent replies should fail closed. fileciteturn11file11 So `v0.0.217` fixed the **wrong-conversation acceptance** bug, but still leaves a **confusing answer-source contract**: ```text ask_response.answer != validated reply text ``` That should be made explicit. ## Correct fix I would build a narrow `v0.0.218` from `v0.0.217`, not accept `v0.0.217` yet. Scope: ```text v0.0.218 — protocol answer-source normalization ``` Required behavior: ```text - When --protocol --parse-reply succeeds: - top-level reply remains authoritative - ask_response.answer must not contain a stale/intermediate stream placeholder as if it were final - preserve browser/intermediate text separately for diagnostics ``` Recommended output shape: ```json { "ask_response": { "ok": true, "answer": "BEGIN_PROMPTBRANCH_REPLY_JSON ... END_PROMPTBRANCH_REPLY_JSON", "answer_source": "validated_protocol_reply", "stream_answer_preview": "Thinking\n\nStreaming interrupted...", "stream_answer_final": false } } ``` Or safer: ```json { "ask_response": { "ok": true, "answer": null, "answer_source": "protocol_reply_authoritative", "raw_browser_answer_preview": "Thinking\n\nStreaming interrupted...", "raw_browser_answer_complete": false } } ``` The second is cleaner because it prevents accidental downstream parsing of `ask_response.answer`. ## Acceptance rule for `v0.0.218` The smoke should pass only if: ```text - status = reply_validated - reply_status = no_artifact - reply_validation_ok = true - ask_response.answer is either: - the validated protocol reply text, OR - null with answer_source explaining that top-level reply is authoritative - raw/intermediate browser text is preserved only as diagnostic metadata - no artifact/source/adoption behavior changed ``` ## Verdict ✅ Strengths — `v0.0.217` proved the core protocol path and fixed the wrong-conversation regression. ⚠️ Weaknesses — it still exposes a misleading `ask_response.answer`, which is unsafe for automation and diagnostics. 🔍 Unknowns — whether any existing commands/tests expect `ask_response.answer` to always be a string. 🧩 Next step — do **not accept `v0.0.217` yet**; implement `v0.0.218` as a narrow protocol answer-source normalization release.

